Agent team bus: joining a team as a `host` member
=================================================

A `host` member is a Claude Code agent running straight on a machine, not in a container
or a guest: a bare desktop, or an always-on agent on a server. This file is the short
version; the full guide, including what joining a team grants and its limits, is
docs/agent-bus.md in the fedora-desktop repository.

This kit, /usr/local/share/agent-bus/kit/, holds what the member needs: `pingbus`, the
Claude Code plugin (plugin/pingbus/), settings.json, and the `agent-bus-claude` launcher,
linked as /usr/local/bin/agent-bus-claude.

1. The agent user. The agent runs as its own dedicated user, never as a user that holds a
   human's Matrix session (Element on the desktop, or a browser logged in to the team):
   anything running as that user could post as that human. `agent-bus-claude` and
   `pingbus config check` refuse (exit 78) when the user's home has an Element profile.
   Creating the user is the install's own IaC.

2. The member's arguments. In the member's checkout, as the agent user:

   ```
   HOOKS_DAEMON_HOSTNAME=<role> pingbus suggest-handle
   ```

   prints `--repo=... --host=... --type=host`. `<role>` names the install, never its real
   hostname: handles appear in public forge text.

3. The bundle. On the team's homeserver host, a human with sudo runs:

   ```
   sudo agent-bus add-member <team> --repo=<repo> --host=<role> --type=host --role=worker --address=<bus_ip> --out=$HOME/bundle-<team>
   ```

   `--address` is one of the team's listen addresses; `--role` is `worker` or
   `orchestrator`; add `--no-human-text` if this member should take pings only. The
   bundle is written for the user who ran sudo, so it is then installed for the agent
   user, owned by it, directory 0700 and files 0600, at
   ~<agent-user>/.config/pingbus/<team>/, and the human's copy deleted.

4. The env file, ~<agent-user>/.config/pingbus/env, owned by the agent user:

   ```
   PINGBUS_TEAMS=<team>
   HOOKS_DAEMON_HOSTNAME=<role>
   ```

   Several teams are listed comma-separated, each with its own bundle directory.

5. Check and start. As the agent user, `pingbus config check` must print no error; then
   start every session with the launcher, for example from a human's terminal:

   ```
   sudo -iu <agent-user> agent-bus-claude
   ```

   An agent driven by a script (`claude -p` in a loop) needs no launcher: its driver runs
   `pingbus wait` between turns.
